From: WallView
Subject: [PATCH] ThreadedScrollingTree: commit layer positions on every display
 refresh with coordinated graphics

displayDidRefreshOnScrollingThread() only applied layer positions when
the tree was not in the Idle synchronization state. In Idle it relied on
the main thread: either a rendering update was already scheduled
(m_renderingUpdateWasScheduled, then a 1 ms timer desynchronizes), or
nothing happened until the main thread ran
scrollingThreadAddedPendingUpdate(). Kinetic scrolling changes the scroll
position in serviceScrollAnimations(), after the flag was last set, and
wheel events handled while the main thread is busy cannot set it either,
so a main thread task that started in the Idle state froze the scrolled
layers until the main thread was free again (40 ms of script = 2-3
missed frames on the NSPanel), although wheel events, display refresh
notifications and the compositor did not depend on the main thread.

With coordinated graphics the scrolling thread only updates a layer
property and asks the threaded compositor for a frame, so do that on
every display refresh, whatever the synchronization state. A position
that did not change does not request a composition.

WALLVIEW_WEBKIT_NO_SCROLL_COMMIT (any value) restores the old behaviour
for A/B measurements.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/Source/WebCore/page/scrolling/ThreadedScrollingTree.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/page/scrolling/ThreadedScrollingTree.cpp
@@ -513,7 +513,13 @@
     m_lastDisplayDidRefreshTime = now;
     serviceScrollAnimations(now);
 
-    if (m_state != SynchronizationState::Idle && canUpdateLayersOnScrollingThread())
+#if USE(COORDINATED_GRAPHICS)
+    static bool isDisabled = !!getenv("WALLVIEW_WEBKIT_NO_SCROLL_COMMIT");
+    bool shouldApplyLayerPositions = isDisabled ? m_state != SynchronizationState::Idle : true;
+#else
+    bool shouldApplyLayerPositions = m_state != SynchronizationState::Idle;
+#endif
+    if (shouldApplyLayerPositions && canUpdateLayersOnScrollingThread())
         applyLayerPositionsInternal();
 
     switch (m_state) {
